It took some work to get all the packages to install and work properly, and the code to run cleanly, but I managed to do it... I'd suggest in the future including code that will all run when copied and pasted into a new R session (this includes loading the data). Also, it helps to know what files go where, and what the working directory should be.

Anyway, I got this to work in ggplot2 version 0.9.0, which should be officially released tomorrow. I think that the problem was solved by the inherit.aes=FALSE argument, which tells the new geom not to inherit the default aesthetic mappings -- those from ggplot(div0A_merged, aes(long, lat, group=group)). The OTc data frame doesn't have those columns, and so when it inherits those mappings and tries to use them, it has problems.

For the new version of ggplot2, I had to change the way color and alpha are specified. There are probably some other small things I forgot...

library(ggplot2)

OT1 <- read.csv('OT1.csv')

OTz<-OT1[OT1$TSUM==0,]#selecting only my zeros
OTc<-OT1[OT1$TSUM!=0,]

# plotting data with ggplot2 (see attached figure)
library(scales)
v <- ggplot(OTc, aes(longitude, latitude, size=TSUM)) +
  geom_point(colour="red", alpha=0.1) + facet_wrap(~month, ncol=2)
v + geom_point(data = OTz,size = 1,colour = "black", alpha=0.2) +

  opts(title="Otter trawl 1996-2011")

library(rgdal)

library(sp)
library(maptools)
gpclibPermit()

div0A <- readOGR(dsn=".", layer="Projections")

names(div0A)
dim(div0A)

library(gpclib)

# add the 'id' variable to the shapefile and use it to merge both files
div0A@data$id = rownames(div0A@data)
div0A.df <- as.data.frame(div0A)# convert shapefile to dataframe

div0A_fort <- fortify(div0A, region="id")# fortify to plot with ggplot2
head(div0A_fort)

# Merge shapefile and the as.dataframe shapefile

library(plyr)

div0A_merged <- join(div0A_fort, div0A.df, by="id")
head(div0A_merged)

# Graph with the shapefile
p <- ggplot(div0A_merged, aes(long, lat, group=group)) +

  geom_polygon(aes(data=div0A_merged)) +
  geom_path(color="white") + theme_bw()

# With points added
p + geom_point(aes(x=longitude, y=latitude, size=TSUM), data=OTc,
         colour="red", alpha=0.2, inherit.aes=FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~month, ncol=2)

